<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thomas Langley</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1363–<a href="/years/1437">1437</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thomas Langley (c. 1363–1437) was an English churchman and royal servant who was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Durham from 1406 to 1437 and twice Lord Chancellor, under Henry IV and Henry V. As bishop he governed the County Palatine of Durham through its own courts and officials, and he restored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a> chapel of Durham Cathedral, where he is buried.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bishops such as Antony Bek and <b>Thomas Langley</b> were leading royal servants.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-palatine-of-durham">County Palatine of Durham</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Durham, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.8,-2.6&amp;year=1411" aria-label="Lancashire on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4492 316 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Thomas Langley"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4942 586h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4942 586h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.8,-2.6&amp;year=1411">What was here in 1411? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
